* Background sensitivity (A2, `background_*.csv`): B20, B200 and kmeans50 on German (RF 10, GBM 20, MLP 20 instances) and ACS-Employment (30 instances each), `shap_kernel` `l1_reg` False and auto at m = 1024 and 2d+2048; all jobs finished. LR is not in A2 (its background effect is in floor b by B). `background_reference_shift.csv` is the shift of the reference itself against the B = 50 draw, **not** floor b; floor b as a function of B is `floor_b_by_background.csv` (K independent draws per B on the same instances as floor a).
* RF log-odds vs probability output (A1 robustness of the explained output): **not run**. All RF values are of P(class 1); the log-odds variant would need new exact tables (≈ 10 CPU-minutes per German RF instance) and was not computed.
* Pima RF: E1 (exact Shapley of the sklearn model's own predictions on the stored table) and interventional TreeSHAP differ by median 8e-4, max 4e-2 relative L2 (`validity.csv`, check `E1_exact_vs_E2_treeshap`); every other RF/GBM cell agrees to ≤ 4e-6. Pima has integer-valued features and exact value = split-threshold ties; a strict-vs-≤ traversal of the trees did not reproduce TreeSHAP, so the cause is unresolved. E1 is the reference throughout; TreeSHAP is a cross-check only.
* **LR at small m is a bias/ill-conditioning result, not variance.** LR is additive, so any well-posed estimate is exact and its run-to-run spread is zero (`validity.csv`, `run_to_run_sd_over_norm_phi`). Nonzero spread occurs only where shap's weighted regression is under-determined or ill-conditioned: m = 32 on German (1198 of 1200 runs) and ACS-Employment, one run of 1200 at German m = 64 (instance 31), Pima m = 13 (and m = 25). The `reading` column of `validity.csv` labels these rows; P_c and agreement of LR at m < 3d must not be read as sampling variance.
* `results/retime_*.json`: timing probes (fit time, seconds per 2·10^5 row-evaluations, exact-table CPU seconds) that fixed the cut of the grid; the AUC printed there is of the probe fit, the delivered AUC is `cells.csv:model_auc`.

## M5: slope of m*(g) and the probit check of H3
* `mstar_robust.csv` (one row per `variant` × cell; `pc_exact`, `shap_kernel` `l1_reg=False`, `kernel_paired`, `permutation_antithetic`; 100 bootstrap resamples) re-fits the Tobit slope under: `full` (the grid of `mstar_tobit.csv`), `thin2_off0`/`thin2_off1` (every second budget, i.e. a ×2 coarser grid, from the 1st / 2nd budget), `thin3_off0` (every third budget), `stable` (m* = the smallest budget from which P_c ≥ 0.9 holds at it and at every larger budget). Variants are absent where fewer than 3 budgets remain (d ≤ 10 grids have 5). Reading (non-linear models): `stable` equals `full` to two decimals (a single noisy crossing does not move the slope); `permutation_antithetic` stays at −1.4…−2.1 under every variant; `shap_kernel` stays at −0.4…−1.5 (never near −2); `kernel_paired` is grid-dependent: −1.4…−1.8 on the full grid and on `thin2`, but −0.65…−1.1 on `thin3_off0` (a ×3 coarser grid leaves few budgets between the censoring limits). The slope of the m*-vs-margin law is therefore robust to the grid for the first and third estimators and not for `kernel_paired`.
* `mstar_tobit.csv` is the primary slope estimate (interval-censored Gaussian regression of log m* on log margin; the exact φ-based margin g). Assumes Gaussian residuals; right- and left-censored instances are counted.
* `probit_fit.csv` fits, over all (instance, budget) points of a cell with R > 1, n_correct ~ Binomial(R, Φ(β·x)), x = g_abs·√m_eff/σ. g_abs = |φ_a| − |φ_b| for the boundary pair a, b (3rd and 4th feature by exact |φ|) and D = |φ̂_a| − |φ̂_b|, the quantity whose sign decides the boundary. σ is a per-instance noise scale: `sigma_loo` = leave-one-budget-out median over the other budgets with m_eff ≥ 256 of SD(D)·√m_eff across the R runs (all three estimators), or `sigma_plugin` = plug-in SE of D times √m_eff from the estimator's own covariance, covariance between the two features ignored (`kernel_paired` and `permutation_antithetic` only). H3 predicts β = 1 (collapse). `beta_free`/`alpha_free` free the intercept, `beta_g_only` fits g√m alone without σ (`nll_gain_vs_g_only`). Implied slope: `slope_logmstar_vs_logg` = −2 + 2·slope(log σ vs log g) (`slope_logsigma_vs_logg`). CI by instance bootstrap (300 resamples); `ci_hi = 20` is the upper bound of the search (separation: every point at large x is correct), not an estimate.
* Reading (ranges over the cells, `sigma_loo` and `sigma_plugin` rows together): `permutation_antithetic` collapses (β 0.78–0.99 over all budgets, 0.93–1.05 at m_eff ≥ 256). `shap_kernel` collapses only at large budgets (β 0.90–1.04 at m_eff ≥ 1024 on d ≥ 16; 0.78–0.89 at m_eff ≥ 256; 0.18–0.69 over all budgets, d ≥ 16 0.18–0.33). `kernel_paired` stays below 1 (β 0.00–0.54 over all budgets, 0.37–0.75 at m_eff ≥ 256, 0.69–0.85 at m_eff ≥ 1024). In both sampling designs the fit over all budgets is dominated by the smallest budgets, where SD(D) does not follow 1/√m, so σ_loo underestimates the noise there; for `kernel_paired` the plug-in σ also ignores the finite-population effect at m up to half of 2^d (conjecture, not tested). For `kernel_paired` on Pima, β ≈ 0 with a log-likelihood equal to the null model: x carries no information and the row must not be read. The implied slope of log m* vs log g is −1.6 to −2.1 over cells (−2 + 2·slope(log σ vs log g)): where σ is uncoupled from g the slope is about −2, whereas the g-only interpolated slope is shallower (`mstar_fit.csv`, `mstar_tobit.csv`).

## R1: certificate vs run-twice
Certificate: one `kernel_paired` run with 2m coalitions gives φ̂ and the plug-in covariance (Covert & Lee); `score_certificate` = Monte Carlo (200 draws) probability that the top-3
set of φ̃ ~ N(φ̂, Σ̂) equals that of φ̂. Run-twice: two independent runs with m coalitions each; `score_run_twice` = overlap of their top-3 sets, delivered run = the first.
Evaluations: certificate (2m+1)·50+1, run-twice 2·((m+1)·50+1) (the competitor spends 51 evaluations more). Outcome = exact top-3 set of the delivered run (`correct_*`). Scores are
mapped to probabilities by logistic calibration on (score, log2 m), cross-fitted: `heldout_instance` (within dataset × model, folds by instance id mod 5) and `loo_dataset`
(fitted on the other datasets). Brier per instance (mean over m and runs), paired difference certificate − run-twice with instance bootstrap CI; useful margin 0.01.
`certificate_coverage.csv` is the variance-estimator check (share of exact φ_j inside φ̂_j ± 1.96 SE). `certificate_budget_rule.csv`: stop at the smallest m with calibrated probability ≥ 0.9
(certificate) or agreeing runs (run-twice); compares used evaluations and achieved share correct with a flat budget.

How to read the R1 contrasts. `check_vs_run_twice` is the pre-registered primary contrast: the target of each method is the correctness of the run it
delivers (certificate: the 2m-coalition run; run-twice: the first of the two m-coalition runs). The two delivered runs differ in accuracy (`base_rate` in
`certificate_summary.csv`), and Brier depends on the base rate, so `brier_skill` (1 − Brier / p(1−p)) is also given. `check_vs_run_twice_avg` is the variant in
which run-twice delivers the mean of its two runs, which matches the certificate's deliverable (same base rate within 0.01); a negative difference favours the check. **The `_avg` variant is post hoc**: the pre-registered primary contrast is `check_vs_run_twice` (run-twice delivers its first run); `_avg` was added after the results of the primary contrast were seen, to remove the base-rate mismatch it showed. Both are reported with `base_rate_certificate` and `base_rate_other` (the share correct of the delivered run, averaged over instances, budgets and runs) and the two Briers beside every contrast row of `certificate_paired_diff.csv`. The two contrasts disagree in sign on the pooled rows (`m = all`): the primary contrast favours run-twice (its first run is the less accurate deliverable, so its Brier is lower), the matched one favours the check.

`certificate_budget_rule.csv`: `evals_saved_frac` is the relative saving of the rule against a flat run with the same share correct (interpolated linearly in log2 m on the flat grid; NaN where that share is outside the flat grid), with an instance-bootstrap 95 % CI (2000 resamples; `n_boot_defined` resamples had a defined flat comparison, so the CI is from those and can be present when the point estimate is NaN) and `model = all` rows pooling the four models of a dataset (the paired CI behind "about 25 % fewer evaluations": German 20 % [17, 25], ACS-Employment 23 % [18, 28], d ≤ 10 datasets 1–2 %). Run-twice rows have negative savings: its stop rule uses more evaluations than a flat run for the same share correct. German GBM has all 40 instances here.
Labels: `check_better` (CI below 0 and at most −0.01), `*_below_margin` (CI excludes 0, |diff| < 0.01), `run_twice_better`, `not_resolved`.
